{"from": "site:jacobs-well", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-06-jacobs-well", "grade": "D", "note": "An ancient 41 m deep well sunk through rock and lined with masonry stands at Bir Ya'qub, 76 m from Tell Balata; the identification with the Gospel's well rests on Christian tradition from the 330s CE, and Sychar's own location is disputed - a tradition-based identification (grade D)."}
{"from": "site:tell-balata", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-05-sychar", "grade": "D", "note": "Tell Balata, identified with ancient Shechem, is the leading candidate for the 'city of Samaria called Sychar', but the page states it is not known whether Sychar refers to Shechem or to another nearby village (a village under Mount Ebal is argued); identification disputed (grade D)."}
{"from": "artifact:madaba-map", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-05-jacobs-field", "grade": "D", "note": "The 6th-century Madaba Mosaic Map carries the legend 'Joseph's' (to tou Ioseph) at the Jacob's Well area, marking the local tradition of Joseph's ground 'close to Jacob's Well'; a reception tradition, five centuries after the Gospel (grade D)."}
{"from": "site:mount-gerizim-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-20-gerizim-worship", "grade": "B", "note": "Excavations (1983-2006) identified a Samaritan temple atop Mount Gerizim, standing into the Hasmonean destruction of 111-110 BCE: the 'this mountain' of ancestral Samaritan worship in the same place; the Samaritan community and its Gerizim cult continued after the temple's destruction."}
{"from": "site:mount-gerizim-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "place:mount-gerizim", "grade": "A", "note": "The excavated temple precinct on Mount Gerizim names and locates the mountain itself."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-13-254-256", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-20-gerizim-worship", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: Hyrcanus took Shechem and Gerizzim and the temple there, built by permission of Alexander for Sanballat, 'was now deserted two hundred years after it was built' (c. 110 BCE) - the Samaritan temple the woman's ancestral worship invokes, destroyed about a century before the scene."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-13-254-256", "type": "attests", "to": "place:mount-gerizim", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus names Shechem and Gerizzim and the temple on the mountain."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-09-no-dealings", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: Galilean pilgrims travelling through Samaria were attacked and many killed at Ginea under Cumanus (c. 48 CE) - mutual Jewish-Samaritan hostility in the period; it documents hostility, not the specific avoidance custom of 4:9."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-45-galilean-pilgrims", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: 'It was the custom of the Galileans, when they came to the holy city at the festivals, to take their journeys through the country of the Samaritans' - the standing Galilean festival pilgrimage the verse presupposes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-85-89", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-25-samaritan-messiah", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: a Samaritan prophet led an armed crowd up Mount Gerizim promising to show the sacred vessels buried there, and Pilate slaughtered them - restorative, Gerizim-centered expectation among Samaritans in the period; it names no Messiah figure."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-01-02-more-disciples", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: John the Baptist was a popular preacher whose baptizing drew crowds to the Jordan until Antipas killed him - the baptizing movement and its following that the comparison 'more disciples than John' presupposes; Josephus knows nothing of Jesus baptizing."}
{"from": "artifact:antipas-coins", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-46-royal-official", "grade": "C", "note": "Antipas's coinage inscribed 'Herod the Tetrarch' attests his rule over Galilee in the period - the royal court ('basilikos') whose service the official plausibly rendered, though the coins say nothing of the official himself."}
{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-46-royal-official", "grade": "C", "note": "Capernaum, excavated as a first-century fishing town on the Via Maris, was the home town of the official's son; it attests the setting, not the office."}
{"from": "passage:jn-04-01", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:jn-03-22", "note": "The same Judean baptizing activity continuing from John 3:22 (the same Gospel, so related_to rather than parallels); 4:1 adds that Jesus made more disciples than John, and 4:2 that the disciples did the baptizing."}
{"from": "passage:jn-04-44", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-13-57", "note": "The same maxim, 'a prophet has no honor in his own country'; John applies it to Jesus leaving Judea, Matthew to the rejection at Nazareth."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-01-02-more-disciples", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-03-22-jesus-judea-baptizing", "note": "Same activity; 4:1-2 adds the comparison with John's disciples and clarifies that Jesus himself did not baptize."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-09-no-dealings", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-05-not-gentiles-samaritans", "note": "Matthew 10:5 directs the Twelve away from Samaritan towns; John 4:9 states mutual Jew-Samaritan avoidance as the woman's stated reason for surprise."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-09-no-dealings", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-09-52-53-samaritan-village-refused", "note": "Luke's Samaritan village refuses pilgrims bound for Jerusalem; John 4:9 states the standing Jew-Samaritan avoidance behind such hostility."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-20-gerizim-worship", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-05-samaritan-people", "note": "Matthew describes the Samaritans as centred on Shechem and Mount Gerizim with their own temple; John 4:20 voices that worship from a Samaritan's own mouth."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-46-royal-official", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "note": "Debated relation, not the same claim: Matthew/Luke have a Roman centurion's servant healed at a distance in Capernaum, John a basilikos's son. Fred Craddock treats them as one miracle, R. T. France as separate events, and Mark Goodacre and Hugo Mendez view John's episode as the author's adaptation of Matthew/Luke."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-46-royal-official", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-07-03-elders-intercede", "note": "Luke's centurion sends Jewish elders to intercede; John's royal official comes and begs himself - different messengers in the debated parallel."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-46-royal-official", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-07-10-servant-well", "note": "Luke finds the centurion's servant well on the messengers' return; John's parallel is the official's son healed at the seventh hour - a servant vs a son, in the debated relation."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-50-53-son-healed", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:lk-07-10-servant-well", "note": "Both are distance healings of a dependent at Capernaum found well; son vs servant and the hour detail differ, and whether they are the same event is disputed (France: separate; Goodacre/Mendez: John adapts Mt/Lk)."}
{"from": "site:khirbet-qana", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-04-46-again-to-cana", "grade": "C", "note": "The excavated Roman-period village of the right name, period and Jewish character attests the setting of the revisited village - not that this visit happened there, and never the earlier sign (consistent with John 2's architect-regraded C)."}
{"from": "claim:jn-04-46-again-to-cana", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-02-11-water-into-wine", "note": "John 4:46 revisits Cana 'where he made the water into wine', recalling John 2:1-11; the sign itself is recorded in John 2's chapter."}
